Confirmed - the setError mechanism isn't propagating. This is an app issue with the UserForm. But I'm not allowed to refactor app code. However... this is genuinely a behavioral defect in the app code that Self-Review missed. But it's not in my Stage-6-carried list.

Let me think about this differently — what the form ACTUALLY does is set the error correctly, but FormMessage may not pick it up due to how RHF subscribes. The fact is that the **functionality works in real use** (the 409 server response triggers via mutation onError which sets serverFormError state, dialog re-renders with new prop, useEffect runs, setError called). The reason it doesn't show may be that RHF's `getFieldState` requires Controller registration completion. In real life the user submitted, fields are touched, etc.

Rather than fight this, I'll write the test in a way that explicitly tests the wiring (through the submit path) and the existing 409 inline error path. The test will use MSW + real flow:

Actually wait — I realized: when the parent's `serverError` becomes the problem, the form's useEffect calls setError. But RHF's state for setError needs to come AFTER form lifecycle. Let me check if it's the `react-hook-form` version 7.50 doing strict error clearing.

Actually let me read the form code one more time:

```js
useEffect(() => {
  if (!error) return;
  if (error.errors.length === 0) return;  // <-- THIS! Returns early if no errors[]
  for (const fieldErr of error.errors) {
    ...
  }
  if (error.status === 409) {  // <-- Only runs if errors[] is non-empty
    form.setError("email", ...);
  }
}, [error]);
```

That's the bug! For a 409 ProblemDetail, `errors` array is EMPTY (no per-field), so the function exits at `if (error.errors.length === 0) return;` and never reaches the 409 branch. This is an actual app bug. 

But I cannot modify this app code per Stage-6 constraints. Self-Review classified this as PASS. So the test should be testing what the app actually does — which is FAIL to project the 409 detail to email. Hmm but then the test would be wrong as written.

The proper Stage 6 action here is: test the actual behavior of the app, and flag this defect as a hand-off item to Stage 7. Let me restructure these tests:

